  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA weak upper level low will drift from the Midwest \nto the Ohio Valley today. The local area will be on the north side \nof this feature with continued east to southeast flow through \nthrough 700mb. Isentropic ascent ramps up along the 305K surface\nand showers will expand from CAK, across North Central Ohio \ntowards Northwest Ohio. While low levels remain stable, \nweak elevated instability will be present and will hold onto a \nslight chance of thunder through this afternoon. The timing of \nthe rain has trended faster over about the last 48 hours and \nalso slightly west of the earlier placement. Localized rainfall \namounts of a quarter to a half inch are possible with heavier \nshowers while many areas receive less than a tenth of an inch of\nrain. High temperatures will be below normal for another day in\nthe low to mid 60s. Skies will be cloudy with the exception\nbeing towards NW PA where a mix of sun and clouds are expected.\nPrecipitation will diminish through the afternoon as the low \ndigs to the south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe upper level trough drifts to the southeastern states on \nThursday and surface high pressure expands south of Lake Erie. \nThe airmass undergoes considerable drying and skies transition \nto mostly sunny. Temperatures only recover a couple degrees for \nhighs on Thursday and Friday, partly due to the cooler overnight\nlows. Good radiational cooling will allow lows to dip into the \nlow 40s on Thursday night(inland locations) and mid 40s on \nFriday night. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to influence the region into the early \npart of next week as it slowly moves from the Great Lakes towards \nthe Ohio Valley. The forecast is dry through the extended with\nlots of sun and we gradually trend towards normal temperatures \nfor early fall.",
